session:pr698Summary a agp:TranscriptSummary ;
    agp:summaryText "Issue #572 asked for per-operator (per-BGP, per-component) ACTUAL timing in the ?explain=true EXPLAIN endpoint, a follow-up #537 deliberately deferred because it required threading an optional timing sink through sparql_parser's recursive evaluator without regressing the non-explain hot path. Before designing, I read PR #570's summary (#537's implementation) and PR #673's summary (the EvalCtx bundling refactor, #466) per this repo's provenance-check step; #673 confirmed eval_component/eval_components_budgeted were deliberately left outside EvalCtx, which steered the design away from growing EvalCtx and toward a thread-local profiler mirroring the existing CURRENT_BASE pattern (#346) already in execute/mod.rs. An advisor consultation before writing code caught a critical gap in my first-pass design: I had assumed actual runtime timings could be merged positionally into #537's static plan tree, but OPTIONAL/GRAPH bodies run once per outer row (not once) and the static walk's empty-already-bound approximation can diverge from real per-row join order inside OPTIONAL -- so there is no stable 1:1 position to merge into. I redesigned around a separate runtime 'profile' tree, built from the real recursion as it happens, with repeated invocations of the same static body merged into one aggregate node (invocations/totalTimeMs/rowsIn/rowsOut summed). The thread-local profiler uses RAII ScopeGuard (Drop, not a manual pop) specifically so a partial, correctly-scoped profile survives a deadline.check()? early return -- matching #537's existing 'static plan survives a failing execution' contract for the new runtime profile. A single-slot pending_children handoff between a closing scope and its consumer required explicit take-and-merge code in exactly three QueryComponent arms (Optional, Graph -- both invoke their body once per outer row; Union -- invokes left then right, exactly twice) while Minus/Group/Subquery needed none, since I verified by reading their code that each calls its inner evaluator at most once. Writing the tests first caught a real naming/semantics ambiguity I hadn't resolved: should an OPTIONAL's own wrapper node report invocations=3 (matching its merged inner body) or invocations=1 (one component dispatch summarizing 3 rows)? The implementation naturally produced the latter (self-consistent: rowsIn/rowsOut sum across rows, children's invocations reflect the body's real repeat count), so I fixed the test's assertion to match this more precise semantics rather than changing working code. CI's Docs job caught two rustdoc intra-doc links from newly-public items into the private profile module (-D warnings with rustdoc::private_intra_doc_links); fixed by using plain code spans instead of [`...`] links for those two references. Filed #697 (EXISTS/NOT EXISTS and property-path step-level timing) as an explicit follow-up at Status Todo, since eval_filter's EXISTS evaluation runs once per solution row with no existing take-and-merge discipline, and rushing that into this PR's scope risked exactly the kind of stale-pending-children leak the single-slot design is built to avoid." ;
    agp:reasoningFor ghpull:698 ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy session:pr698 ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo session:claudeSonnet5 .

session:pr698Summary agp:decisionPoint session:pr698Decision .

session:pr698Decision a agp:Decision ;
    agp:summaryText "Collect runtime per-operator timing as its own separate 'profile' tree (sibling JSON field to #537's static 'plan'), built from the real execution as it happens via a thread-local profiler with RAII scope guards, rather than merging actual timings positionally into the existing static plan tree." ;
    agp:alternative "Considered merging actual timings into #537's existing PlanNode tree by position (e.g. adding an actualTimeMs field to each PlanNode). Rejected after re-reading explain.rs's own documented limitation: the static walk uses an empty already-bound set at every BGP/OPTIONAL body, which is only exact at the top level and in independently-evaluated scopes (UNION arms, bare Group bodies, MINUS's RHS) but an approximation inside OPTIONAL -- and OPTIONAL/GRAPH bodies are evaluated once per outer row at runtime, not once, so there is no stable 1:1 node position to attach a single actual-timing value to. A separate tree, independently shaped by what actually ran and explicit about merging repeated invocations, avoids baking an unsound positional correspondence into the API." .
